            <p>
              On the morning of August 11, Eboni and her dog, Boone, were home when a severe storm and tornado tore through Columbus, causing significant damage throughout the neighborhood and to the apartment building they called home.
            <p>
              Thankfully, Eboni and Boone made it out safely. But the damage to the building was extensive. City and fire officials ultimately determined that the structure was unsafe, and residents were not permitted to return to their homes.
            </p>
            <p>What began as a frightening morning quickly became the reality of losing not only a home, but many of the belongings and everyday comforts that made it one.</p>
            <p>More Than Just an Apartment</p>
            <p>For Eboni, Olde Towne East has been more than simply the neighborhood where she lived. It has been a community she has grown to love, champion, and feel deeply connected to. Her apartment became a place of refuge and home, and she hopes that as she rebuilds, she can remain connected to the neighborhood and community that have meant so much to her.</p>
            <p>Eboni and Boone’s loss is also just one part of a much larger story. Neighbors throughout the area are navigating damage, displacement, loss, and the uncertainty of what comes next.</p>
            <p>In the middle of all of that, Eboni is beginning the process of rebuilding finding a new home, replacing what was lost, and creating a sense of stability again for herself and Boone.</p>


